A trie (pronounced "try"), or prefix tree, is a tree-like data structure used to store and search strings efficiently. 
Each node holds a single character, and paths spell out words.
It makes operations like autocomplete and spell-checking fast, taking time proportional to the key length.

How a Trie WorksRoot node: 
Starts empty with no character value.Child nodes: 
Each branch represents the next letter in a word.End marker:
A flag on a node shows it finishes a valid word.Shared paths:
Words with the same start use the same nodes.
